\id 2TH - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Thessalonians
\toc1 The Second Letter of Paul to the Assembly in Thessalonica
\toc2 2 Thessalonians
\toc3 2Th
\mt1 2 Thessalonians

\imt Introduction
\ip This is a second letter from Paul, Silvanus, and Timothy to the same community they had written to only a short while before. Thessalonica was the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia, in what is now northern Greece — a busy port on the main road east, and a free city that ran its own affairs. The believers there were mostly Gentiles who had turned from their idols to the God of Israel, and the turn had cost them: their own neighbors had been making them pay for it since the day they made it. The date is the early 50s.
\ip Three things had happened since the first letter. The persecution had not let up. Word had reached the community — through a prophetic utterance, a spoken report, or a letter with the apostles' name on it, Paul does not know which — that the day of the Lord had already arrived. And the problem of members who would not work, raised gently in the first letter, had gotten worse.
\ip The middle item is what provoked this letter. The claim that the day had come did not comfort them; it terrified them. If the Lord had come and the promised reckoning had happened, then they had been passed over — still suffering, still waiting, and now apparently left out of the very thing they had been told to expect. The letter exists to take that fear apart.
\ip So it moves in three steps. Chapter 1 says that their suffering is not evidence God has forgotten them but evidence that his verdict is coming. Chapter 2 says the day has not arrived, and names what must happen before it can. Chapter 3 says how to live in the meantime — which turns out to mean going to work in the morning.

\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\p
\v 1 Paul and Silvanus and Timothy, to the assembly\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl ekklēsia\tl*, the word the Greek scriptures use for Israel gathered before God (Deuteronomy 4:10; 1 Kings 8:14), and also the ordinary word for the citizen assembly of a Greek city.\f* of Thessalonians in God our Father and the Lord Jesus the Christ:
\v 2 favor to you and peace\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl eirēnē\tl*, standing for Hebrew \tl shalom\tl*: not the absence of conflict but wholeness and well-being.\f* from God the Father and the Lord Jesus the Christ.
\s1 Thanks for a Faith Growing Under Persecution
\p
\v 3 We owe thanks to God always concerning you, brothers, as is fitting\f + \fr 1:3 \ft In the first letter thanks was simply given (1 Thessalonians 1:2); here it is owed. Greek \tl opheilomen\tl* is the verb of a debt — what these people have become leaves no choice about it.\f*, because your faithfulness is growing beyond all measure\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl hyperauxanei\tl*, a word found nowhere else in the Greek scriptures: growth that runs past what anyone thought to ask for. In the first letter Paul had prayed to fill up what was still lacking in their faith (1 Thessalonians 3:10). This is the answer, and it overshot the request.\f* and the love of every single one of you toward one another is multiplying,
\v 4 so that we ourselves boast about you among the assemblies of God \add [i.e. we hold you up in front of other communities as what endurance looks like]\add* — over your endurance and faithfulness in all your persecutions and in the afflictions you are bearing up under.\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Faithfulness and love are named; hope, the third of the three in 1 Thessalonians 1:3, is not. Hope is precisely what the false report has shaken (2:1-2), and repairing it is what the next chapter is for.\f*
\s1 God's Justice Will Fall Both Ways
\p
\v 5 This is a plain sign of God's righteous judgment \add [i.e. that you hold up under this at all is proof that his just verdict is already at work]\add*, so that you may be counted worthy of the kingdom of God, for which you are also suffering.
\v 6 Since indeed it is just with God to pay back affliction to those who afflict you\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Greek \tl antapodounai\tl*, the repayment word of Israel's scriptures: "Vengeance is mine, I will repay" (Deuteronomy 32:35 LXX); "repay them according to their deeds" (Psalm 28:4 LXX). The measure is exact — affliction back to those who afflict.\f*,
\v 7 and to you who are afflicted, relief \add [i.e. the pressure lifted off you]\add* along with us, at the uncovering\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Greek \tl apokalypsis\tl*, the pulling away of a covering. The first letter called this event his \tl parousia\tl*, his arrival (1 Thessalonians 4:15); the same event is named here from its other side — the moment he is made visible.\f* of the Lord Jesus from heaven with the angels of his power\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Deuteronomy 33:2; Zechariah 14:5; Daniel 7:10. When God comes, he does not come alone, and neither does he.\f*,
\v 8 in flaming fire\f + \fr 1:8 \ft The exact phrase of the burning bush, where God first showed himself to Moses (Exodus 3:2 LXX), and of God's coming to judge the nations (Isaiah 66:15; Daniel 7:9-10). Fire is what accompanies God's own appearing.\f*, dealing out vengeance to those who do not know God\f + \fr 1:8 \ft "Pour out your wrath on the nations that do not know you" (Jeremiah 10:25 LXX). Not ignorance so much as refusal to reckon with him.\f* and to those who do not obey the good news of our Lord Jesus.
\v 9 They will pay the penalty of everlasting ruin\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl olethros\tl*: ruin, the undoing of everything that makes a life a life (1 Thessalonians 5:3; 1 Corinthians 5:5). \tl Aiōnios\tl* marks it as belonging to the coming age, the counterpart of the "everlasting life" of Daniel 12:2.\f*, from the face of the Lord \add [i.e. shut out from his presence]\add* and from the glory of his strength\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Isaiah 2:10, 19, 21 LXX, almost word for word: men hiding "from the face of the terror of the Lord and from the glory of his strength" on the day he alone is exalted.\f*,
\v 10 when he comes to be glorified in his holy ones \add [i.e. his glory shown in what he has made of them]\add*\f + \fr 1:10 \ft "You are my servant, Israel, in whom I will be glorified" (Isaiah 49:3 LXX). God's glory has never been a solitary display; it is shown through the people who belong to him.\f* and to be marveled at among all who trusted — because our testimony to you was trusted \add [i.e. and you are in that number, since you took our witness for the truth]\add* — on that day.
\s1 A Prayer That He Would Finish What He Began in You
\p
\v 11 To this end we also pray always for you: that our God may count you worthy of the calling, and may bring to fullness every desire for good and every work of faithfulness, in power \add [i.e. by his power, not by the sheer effort of yours]\add*,
\v 12 so that the name of our Lord Jesus may be glorified in you, and you in him \add [— and the honor runs both ways: his name shown in your lives, and your lives lifted into his]\add*, according to the favor of our God and the Lord Jesus the Christ.

\c 2
\s1 The Day of the Lord Has Not Already Come
\p
\v 1 Now we ask you, brothers, concerning the coming of our Lord Jesus the Christ and our being gathered together to him\f + \fr 2:1 \ft Greek \tl episynagōgē\tl*, the ingathering the prophets promised, when God collects his scattered people from wherever they were driven (Deuteronomy 30:3-4; Isaiah 43:5-6; Ezekiel 37:21). It is the same event as 1 Thessalonians 4:15-17, named here not by its scenery but by its heart: gathered to him.\f*,
\v 2 that you not be quickly shaken out of your mind \add [i.e. rocked off the footing you had settled on]\add* or thrown into alarm — not by a spirit, nor by a word, nor by a letter as though from us\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Paul does not say which of the three it was; he covers all of them. But see 3:17 — from here on he signs the close of every letter in his own hand, so that a forgery can be caught.\f* — as though the day of the Lord is already here.\f + \fr 2:2 \ft The day of the Lord is the prophets' name for God's own intervention to judge and to set right (Amos 5:18; Joel 2:1-2; Zephaniah 1:14-16). The claim reaching them is not that it is near — Paul held that it was — but that it has already broken in and they are living inside it. Which would mean the relief promised in chapter 1 had come and gone without them.\f*
\s1 What Must Happen First
\p
\v 3 Let no one deceive you in any way. For it will not come unless the rebellion comes first\f + \fr 2:3 \ft Greek \tl apostasia\tl*, a standing-away. In Israel's scriptures it is the forsaking of God (Joshua 22:22 LXX; Jeremiah 2:19 LXX), and above all the forced abandonment of the covenant under Antiochus, when Jews were compelled to sacrifice to idols and give up the law (1 Maccabees 2:15). Paul writes "the rebellion," with the article — a known item in a sequence he had already taught them (v. 5).\f* and the man of lawlessness is uncovered \add [i.e. the man whose whole character is the throwing off of God's law]\add*, the son of destruction \add [i.e. the one whose end is ruin, settled before he ever appears]\add*,
\v 4 the one who sets himself against and lifts himself over everything called a god or given worship, so that he takes his seat in the sanctuary of God\f + \fr 2:4 \ft Greek \tl naos\tl*, the inner sanctuary where the presence dwells, not the temple complex around it. Behind the picture stands Daniel 11:36, the king who exalts himself above every god; behind that, Antiochus IV, who desecrated the Jerusalem sanctuary (1 Maccabees 1:54). And within living memory, the emperor Gaius ordered his own statue set up in that sanctuary in 40 CE; only his assassination stopped it. Paul elsewhere calls the community itself God's sanctuary (1 Corinthians 3:16), and the words will carry that too.\f*, putting himself on show as being God \add [i.e. not merely thinking it of himself but declaring it in public]\add*.
\v 5 Do you not remember that when I was still with you I was telling you these things \add [— so none of this should have caught you off guard]\add*?
\s1 What Holds Him Back, and How He Ends
\p
\v 6 And now you know what holds him back\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Paul does not say what it is, because he had told them in person and they knew. That teaching is lost, and every reader since has been guessing. What the words themselves establish is only this: something is restraining now, it will be taken out of the way, and its removal is what releases him.\f*, so that he may be uncovered in his own season.
\v 7 For the secret of lawlessness is already at work \add [i.e. what he will one day do in the open is already working out of sight]\add* — only, there is the one who holds back now, until he comes out of the midst \add [i.e. until he is taken out of the way]\add*.
\v 8 And then the lawless one will be uncovered, whom the Lord Jesus will do away with by the breath of his mouth\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Isaiah 11:4 LXX, almost word for word: "with the breath of his lips he shall do away with the wicked one." That is the shoot from the stump of Jesse, Israel's promised king, who kills with a word and needs no battle.\f* and bring to nothing by the shining out of his coming\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Greek \tl epiphaneia\tl*, the word for a god's visible intervention in human affairs, laid over \tl parousia\tl*, the word for a king's state arrival. The mere sight of it finishes him.\f* —
\v 9 whose own coming \add [i.e. his own arrival, staged in mimicry of the Lord's]\add* is by the working of Satan, with all power and signs and wonders of falsehood\f + \fr 2:9 \ft Power, signs, and wonders are the standing description of God's own acts (Deuteronomy 34:11 LXX; Acts 2:22). Deuteronomy 13:1-3 had already faced this: the sign may genuinely come to pass and still be a test. What makes it false is not that it fails but where it leads.\f*,
\v 10 and with every deceit of wrongdoing, for those who are perishing, because they did not welcome the love of the truth \add [i.e. they would not let the truth have them]\add* so as to be rescued.
\v 11 And for this reason God sends them a working that leads astray\f + \fr 2:11 \ft The pattern is old. A lying spirit is sent into the mouth of Ahab's prophets — after Ahab has already jailed the prophet who told him the truth (1 Kings 22:19-23). "If the prophet is deceived, I the LORD have deceived that prophet" (Ezekiel 14:9). Isaiah is sent to a people whose eyes are to be shut (Isaiah 6:9-10). In every case the refusal comes first and the delusion after it — God holding men to what they have already chosen, as in Romans 1:24-28.\f*, so that they trust the lie,
\v 12 so that all may be judged who did not trust the truth but took pleasure in wrongdoing \add [i.e. did not stumble into it but found it agreeable]\add*.
\s1 You Were Taken for Rescue — So Stand Firm
\p
\v 13 But we owe thanks to God always concerning you, brothers loved by the Lord, because God took you for himself as firstfruits\f + \fr 2:13 \ft Greek \tl aparchēn\tl*, the first of the harvest brought to God — the portion that consecrates the whole and says the rest of the crop is coming in (Leviticus 23:10; Romans 16:5). Some manuscripts read \tl ap' archēs\tl*, "from the beginning"; the two are nearly identical in the old capital script.\f* for rescue, in the Spirit's making you holy and in trust of the truth \add [i.e. his Spirit setting you apart, and your own hold on what is true — the two together]\add*.
\v 14 To this he also called you through our good news, for the gaining of the glory of our Lord Jesus the Christ \add [i.e. so that his glory becomes yours to share]\add*.
\v 15 So then, brothers, stand firm and hold fast the traditions you were taught, whether by our word or by our letter.\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Greek \tl paradosis\tl*, teaching handed over intact and meant to be passed on the same way (1 Corinthians 11:2, 23). Against the spirit, the word, and the letter of verse 2, Paul sets what they actually received from him — and spoken and written carry the same weight.\f*
\v 16 Now may our Lord Jesus the Christ himself, and God our Father — who loved us and gave everlasting comfort \add [i.e. comfort that belongs to the coming age and does not run out]\add* and good hope in favor —
\v 17 comfort your hearts and make them firm in every good work and word.

\c 3
\s1 Pray for Us; the Lord Will Hold You Firm
\p
\v 1 For the rest, brothers, pray for us, that the word of the Lord may run\f + \fr 3:1 \ft "He sends out his word to the earth; his word runs swiftly" (Psalm 147:15 LXX). The word is pictured as a runner, not a deposit.\f* and be glorified, just as it did among you,
\v 2 and that we may be rescued from out-of-place and evil men \add [i.e. men who are not merely mistaken but twisted out of true]\add* — for not everyone has faith \add [i.e. some hear the word and answer it with hostility]\add*.
\v 3 But the Lord is faithful, who will make you firm and guard you from the evil one.
\v 4 And we have come to trust in the Lord about you, that what we charge you, you are doing and will do.
\v 5 And may the Lord make your hearts straight toward the love of God and toward the endurance of the Christ \add [i.e. the same staying power he himself showed]\add*.
\s1 Keep Away from the Brother Who Will Not Work
\p
\v 6 Now we charge you, brothers, in the name of our Lord Jesus the Christ, to draw back \add [i.e. pull back from ordinary company with him]\add* from every brother who walks in a disorderly way\f + \fr 3:6 \ft Greek \tl ataktōs\tl*, a soldier's word: out of rank, out of step, the man who has left his place in the formation. Applied to daily life it names someone who has stepped out of the shared order the community lives by.\f* and not according to the tradition they received from us.
\v 7 For you yourselves know how you must imitate us, because we were not disorderly among you,
\v 8 nor did we eat anyone's bread as a gift \add [i.e. take our meals at another man's expense]\add*\f + \fr 3:8 \ft In the Greek cities a traveling teacher normally attached himself to a wealthy patron who fed and housed him, and took his standing from it. Refusing that arrangement was conspicuous, and that was the point.\f*, but in labor and hardship, working night and day so as not to lay a weight on any of you —
\v 9 not because we do not have the right \add [i.e. those who announce the good news may rightly live from it]\add*, but so that we might give you ourselves as a pattern to imitate.
\v 10 For even when we were with you, this is what we kept charging you: if anyone is not willing to work \add [i.e. unwilling, not unable]\add*, neither let him eat.\f + \fr 3:10 \ft At a common table where everyone worked with their hands for modest wages, the man who would not work was eating what others had earned. Cutting off the meal is not punishment for its own sake; it removes the thing that was making the refusal possible.\f*
\s1 Working Their Way into Everyone Else's Business
\p
\v 11 For we hear that some among you are walking in a disorderly way, doing no work of their own but working their way into everyone else's.\f + \fr 3:11 \ft In the Greek the two are the same verb, one of them with a prefix — \tl ergazomenous\tl* and \tl periergazomenous\tl*, working and working-around. They are not idle in the sense of doing nothing; the energy has simply been turned onto other people's affairs. If some of them had believed the report that the day had already come (2:2), giving up a trade would follow from it.\f*
\v 12 To such people we give charge, and we urge them in the Lord Jesus the Christ, that they work quietly and eat their own bread \add [i.e. bread their own hands earned]\add*.
\v 13 But you, brothers, do not grow weary in doing good \add [— do not let a few who will not carry their weight wear down the rest of you]\add*.
\v 14 And if anyone does not obey our word in this letter, mark that man out \add [i.e. let it be plainly known who is refusing]\add*; do not mix yourselves with him, so that he may be put to shame \add [i.e. so that missing your company turns him around]\add*.
\v 15 And do not count him an enemy, but correct him as a brother.
\s1 Peace, and My Own Handwriting
\p
\v 16 Now may the Lord of peace himself give you peace at all times, in every way. The Lord be with all of you.\f + \fr 3:16 \ft "All" — including the brother under discipline. The community withdraws its own company from him; it does not withdraw the Lord's peace, and neither does Paul.\f*
\v 17 The greeting is in my own hand — Paul's. This is the sign in every letter; this is how I write.\f + \fr 3:17 \ft The body of the letter was dictated to a secretary and Paul takes the pen for the closing, as he does elsewhere (1 Corinthians 16:21; Galatians 6:11; Philemon 19). After the letter "as though from us" of 2:2, his handwriting becomes the community's test of anything claiming to be his.\f*
\v 18 The favor of our Lord Jesus the Christ be with all of you.
